// #subtree(
//   title: "spectral space",
//   taxon: "section"
// )[
//   #subtree(
//     title: "quasi-compact",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     - A topological space $X$ is quasi-compact if every open covering of $X$ has a finite subcover. 
//     - A continuous map $f : X -> Y$ is quasi-compact if the preimage $f^(-1)(U)$ of any quasi-compact open $U subset Y$ is quasi-compact.
//     - A subset $Z subset X$ is retrocompact if the inclusion map $Z subset X$ is quasi-compact.
//   ]

//   #subtree(
//     title: "quasi-separated",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     - A topological space $X$ is quasi-separated if the the diagonal $X -> X times X$ is quasi-compact.
//     - A topological space is quasi-separated if and only if intersection $U inter V$ of any quasi-compact opens $U, V$ are still quasi-compact.
//     - A continuous map $f : X -> Y$ is quasi-separated if the diagonal $X -> X times_Y X$ is quasi-compact.
//   ]

//   #subtree(
//     title: "irreducible",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     - A topological space $X$ is irreducible if the intersection $U inter V$ of any non-empty open subsets $U, V$ is still non-empty.
//     - A subset $Z subset X$ is an irreducible component if $Z$ is a maximal irreducible subset of $X$.
//     - A generic point of an irreducible closed subset $Z subset X$ is a point $xi in Z$ such that $Z = overline({xi})$.
//   ]

//   The closure of irreducible subset is still irreducible. In particular, an irreducible component is automatically closed. The closure of a single point is also irreducible.

//   #subtree(
//     title: "Kogolomov",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     A topological space $X$ is Kogolomov if for every distinct points $x, y in X$, there exists $U$ containing one and only one of $x,y$.
//   ]

//   #subtree(
//     title: "sober",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     - A topological space $X$ is quasi-sober if every irreducible closed subset has a generic point.
//     - A topological space $X$ is sober if every irreducible closed subset has a unique generic point
//   ]

//   #subtree(
//     title: "spectral",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     - A topological space $X$ is called spectral if it is sober, quasi-compact, quasi-separated, and the collection of quasi-compact opens forms a basis for the topology.
//     - A continuous map $f : X -> Y$ is called spectral if the preimage $f^(-1)(U)$ of every quasi-compact open $U subset Y$ is quasi-compact.
//   ]

//   #subtree(
//     title: "Hochster",
//     taxon: "theorem"
//   )[
//     A topological space $X$ is spectral if and only if $X$ is the spectrum of a ring if and only if $X$ is an inverse limit of finite Kogolomov topological spaces. Moreover, the category of spectral spaces with spectral maps is equivalent to the pro-category of finite Kogolomov spaces.
//   ]

//   #subtree(
//     taxon: "example"
//   )[
//     Huber's adic spaces $op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ are constructible.
//   ]
// ]

// #subtree(
//   title: "constructible sets",
//   taxon: "section"
// )[
//   #subtree(
//     title: "constructible sets",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     - A subset $E subset X$ is constructible if $E$ is a finite union of $U without V$, where $U, V subset X$ are retrocompact opens.
//     - A subset $E subset X$ is locally constructible if there exists an open covering of $X = union.big V_alpha$ such that $E inter V_alpha$ is constructible in $V_i$.
//   ]

//   A subset $T subset X$ of a spectral space $X$ is constructible if and only if it lies in the Boolean algebra generated by quasi-compact open subsets.

//   #subtree(
//     title: "constructible topology",
//     taxon: "definition"
//   )[
//     - The constructible topology on a spectral space $X$ is the topology generated by constructible subsets. 
//     - A subset $C subset X$ is called pro-constructible if $C$ closed in the constructible topology.
//   ]

//   $X$ equipped with the constructible topology is a profinite sets. Moreover, if $X$ is the inverse limit of finite Kogolomov spaces $X_i$, then $X$ equipped with constructible topology is the inverse limit of finite discrete $X_i$. A subset $C subset X$ is pro-constructible if it's an intersection of closed and open subsets with respect to the constructible topology, which are themselves constructible. Thus $C$ is pro-constructible if and only if $C$ is an intersection of closed subsets.
// ]